import {texEq as eq,inlineMath} from './math.js';
import {section, table, cite} from './topic-tools.js';
const report=()=>cite('https://huggingface.co/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-V2.6-Pro-RL/blob/main/MiMo_V2_6_technical_report.pdf','MiMo V2.6 report');
const comparison=(caption, heads, rows)=>table(caption, heads, rows).replace('<div class="dense-table-wrap">','<div class="dense-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable MiMo comparison">');
export function mimoGrading(link){return section('mimo-grading','MiMo: first create contrast, then allocate credit',`<p>More samples do not guarantee more feedback. If sixteen attempts all pass a binary test, the group-relative signal can vanish. MiMo adds two different grader paths. GRS can create reward differences among passing attempts. GAR allocates existing positive advantage in groups that already have contrast.</p>
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="Binary outcomes split into offline reusable GRS rubrics and online GAR comparisons; both feed the policy update"><div>Binary<br>test outcome</div><span>→</span><div>Offline GRS<br>reusable rubrics</div><span>or</span><div>Online GAR<br>group comparison</div><span>→</span><div>Policy<br>update</div></div>
<p>Groupwise Reward Synthesis jointly examines several offline attempts, the task and repository. Solution rubrics cover correctness, edge cases and codebase fit. Behavior rubrics cover evidence gathering and verification discipline. Individual online trajectories reuse those criteria; the grader need not rebuild them for every sample.</p>
${eq(String.raw`R_i=R_i^{\rm test}S_i^{\rm sol}S_i^{\rm beh}`,'For the stated binary test gate, failure remains zero. Quality factors distinguish passing attempts. Learned rubrics can still encode judge bias.')}
<p>Groupwise Advantage Redistribution instead reads the current group’s specifications, patches, repository state and test outputs. It ranks suitable, precise, minimal changes while checking side effects and craftsmanship. Confirmed hacks receive zero before group statistics are recomputed.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}\lambda&=\frac{\sum_{j\in P}A_j}{\sum_{j\in P}f_jA_j}\\A'_i&=\begin{cases}\lambda f_iA_i&i\in P\\A_i&i\notin P\end{cases}\end{aligned}`,`P contains eligible passing trajectories with positive advantage; ${inlineMath(String.raw`0<f_i\le1`)}. Require a positive denominator. With binary mixed outcomes, passing advantages are positive; soft rewards need an explicit eligibility rule.`)}
<p>The common factor preserves eligible positive mass before any additional cap. A final mean subtraction restores zero group mean. Multiplying zero advantages cannot rescue an all-pass binary group. A cap or broader soft-reward eligibility changes the conservation argument. <a href="${link('post-training/agents')}#gar-conservation">Agent grading →</a> includes the interactive allocation model.</p>
<p>The reported GAR ablation restrains uncontrolled turn and token growth while pass rate improves. Its groups and trajectory lengths change during training. This is useful evidence under the stated setup, not a perfectly compute-matched isolation of every component.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\mathcal L\propto-\frac1G\sum_{i=1}^G\frac1{T_i}\sum_{t=1}^{T_i}r_{i, t}M_{i, t}A_i\log\pi_\theta(a_{i, t}\mid s_{i, t})`,'r is current/behavior token likelihood ratio; M is the local mask. Average tokens within each response before averaging the prompt group. This displayed weighted-log form is not the PPO min surrogate; exact gradient detachment and clipping need the implementation contract.')}
<p>Response normalization prevents extra length alone from increasing a trajectory’s share. Local behavioral regularization addresses malformed calls, unavailable tools, garbled text and repetition. Positive flagged spans lose credit to clean spans; negative flagged spans receive more blame. Infrastructure failure is a separate exclusion, not evidence that a model action was incorrect.</p>
<p>Four sign-specific lower/upper ratio bounds begin with [0.2,5.0] and adapt to entropy. Low entropy permits more positive movement and constrains negative movement; high entropy does the converse. This feedback changes the update region, not the test verifier. <a href="${link('post-training/rl')}#recipe-mimo">Reinforcement Learning →</a> owns the related policy-update controls. ${report()} §§4–5.</p>`);}
export function mimoEvidence(link){return section('mimo-evidence','MiMo: keep failure, transfer and cost evidence together',`<p>Pro and Flash use hidden widths 6,144 and 4,096 respectively. The first global block uses a dense FFN; subsequent sparse FFNs have no shared expert. The 128-position local window bounds those layers’ reachable KV exchange during context parallelism, not total communication or periodic global-attention work.</p>
<p>The 681M vision encoder alternates row-major and column-major serialization so narrow windows connect both image axes across layers. Audio uses discrete residual-quantized codes at 25 Hz and continuous patch features at 6.25 Hz. The different rates describe different representations, not a single interchangeable stream.</p>
<p>Pretraining broadens knowledge, perception, tool conventions and failure recovery before RL exploration. Context grows 32K→256K, then agent mid-training extends to 1M. Muown controls matrix row norms; embeddings, head and router use AdamW. QAT prepares MXFP4 experts for rollout arithmetic. More initial competence changes what the policy can discover, without establishing that the disclosed token mixture is optimal.</p>
<p>Tasks include real issue/PR repairs, workflow/specification/source-code synthesis and longer horizons. Deterministic local files and mocked software expose API, CLI, GUI or MCP interfaces. OSS-Fuzz reproductions use sanitizer evidence. Task, environment and verifier form one object; a plausible instruction with an unreliable checker supplies unreliable learning. <a href="${link('post-training/agents')}#mimo">Agent environments →</a> keeps the detailed task mixture and harness-transfer context.</p>
<p>R3 replays expert indices; top-p replay restores the exact candidate set before probability renormalization. Quantize/dequantize after each update aligns MXFP4 arithmetic. These controls address route, support and arithmetic mismatch separately. None turns an arbitrarily stale trajectory into a fresh on-policy sample.</p>
<p>With a trainable router, load CV rises 0.78→2.0, peak load 6×→16× and cold experts 0.5%→22% within twenty steps. Restoring only the router restores balance without losing benchmark quality. Freezing it stabilizes this run. That intervention supports causality here, not a universal prohibition on router learning. <a href="${link('models/moe')}#rl-routing">MoE routing →</a> holds the mechanism and replay context.</p>
${comparison('Operational failures in the reported run',['Failure','Why the learner cannot ignore it'],[['GPU memory double-bit errors','Device failures interrupt work'],['Kubernetes cyber-cluster failure','Environment availability changes'],['Unreachable grader','Collected trajectories cannot supply finalized rewards'],['Stale length estimates after restart','GPU and host KV pools can exhaust'],['One rank receives >30× mean microbatch tokens','Expert imbalance concentrates memory and work'],['Host OOM during packing','Longer trajectories change host demand']])}
<p>Thirty Pro steps require 123.1 hours; Flash requires 81.8 hours. Those are run durations, not per-trajectory latency or a compute-matched architecture comparison. Restart recovery must restore mixer deficits, length estimates and sample provenance. Across 25 sources, 90× generated-token and 66× active-duration variation makes fixed concurrency a biased data mixer. Adaptive concurrency, deficit scheduling, predicted KV demand and limited recovery replay preserve the intended distribution.</p>
<p>Sample→Sequence→Context→Segment separates branches, compacted contexts and trainable spans. The external Agent Loop owns environment setup, tools, grading and cleanup. The driver schedules metadata; bulk tokens, log probabilities, routes, top-p sets and images are stored once for their consumers. Driver memory otherwise becomes the batch ceiling. <a href="${link('post-training/agents')}#lifecycle">Trajectory lifecycle →</a> explains the state boundaries.</p>
<p>Reward defense combines correct-use mid-training, cleanup of logs, patches, caches, bytecode and post-base Git references, network isolation, iterative hacking-agent probes, offline suspicious-success audits and online hack rejection. The reported confirmed-hack rate below 2% depends on that detection process. It does not bound undetected attacks.</p>
<p>MOPD2 uses full student trajectories where rewards support them. Elsewhere, Teacher-Prefix and SFT-Prefix histories supply assistant decision points. The student generates only the next turn; domain teachers score that student continuation. Demonstrations become contexts, not fixed target answers. This avoids replaying all earlier turns and exposes teachers to student mistakes in game development, research and embodied tasks with expensive or unavailable terminal verification. <a href="${link('post-training/distillation')}">Distillation →</a> supplies the general student-prefix mechanism.</p>
${comparison('Open 9B distilled checkpoint, before → after domain GRPO',['Evaluation','Before','After'],[['SWE-bench Verified','61.1','66.2'],['Terminal-Bench 2.1','37.1','52.8'],['MiMo Cyber Bench mini','31.3','47.0'],['MiMo Visual Coding mini','64.0','72.4']])}
<p>MiMo-V2.6-Distill-Qwen-9B uses 77.4B distilled tokens, 27.2B contributing to SFT loss. The report says domain GRPO improves all eleven reported evaluations; these four examples do not stand in for an independently checked full benchmark census. Roughly 7K released environments, composable mini-harnesses, verifiers and an end-to-end RL framework permit smaller-scale inspection. The result supports transfer below trillion-parameter scale, not isolation of every frontier component.</p>
<p>DFlash’s five-layer dense windowed drafter predicts a future block from backbone anchor features. Draft interactions are bidirectional; context access is up to 1,024 preceding backbone positions. Six drafts are the RL default. After adaptation on early rollouts, reported FP8 per-node throughput improves about 10.3% on that long-context workload. End-to-end throughput, not accepted length alone, selects the useful block size. <a href="${link('execution/speculation')}#dflash">Speculative Decoding →</a> explains target verification.</p>
<p>The report supports sustained mixed-domain gains, held-out-harness transfer, the router intervention and smaller distilled experiments. It does not isolate every combined technique. Frontier comparisons retain harness/evaluation differences; learned graders add bias; disclosed dollars are report-specific economics. Iterative capability gains do not establish autonomous recursive self-improvement in the strong sense. Useful scale depends jointly on exploration breadth, feedback resolution, throughput and stable updates—not a dimensionally valid multiplication of four measured quantities. ${report()} §§6–11 · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/XiaomiMiMo/MiMo-V2.6-Pro-RL','Official model repository')}.</p>`);}
